  "codebook": {
    "definition": "Credit-claiming refers to a senator acting so as to generate a belief among constituents or a relevant audience that they are personally responsible for causing the government to do something that is considered desirable. This usually involves securing material benefits, projects, grants, or favorable policy achievements for their home state, or successfully passing specific amendments or bills.",
    "clarification": "To classify a text as credit-claiming (1), the senator must explicitly link themselves, their staff, or their committee work to a positive outcome. Common inclusion criteria include: 1) Announcing federal grants, funding, or projects awarded to the home state while emphasizing the senator's role in securing or supporting the program. 2) Pointing to successfully passed amendments or bills authored or co-sponsored by the senator. 3) Describing personal interventions, letters, or lobbying efforts that successfully pressured an agency or official to resolve an issue.",
    "positive_examples": [
      {
        "document_id": "1862",
        "explanation": "The senators explicitly claim credit for delivering $12.5 million in vital military projects, stating they 'delivered on their promise' and 'went to bat' for the funding."
      },
      {
        "document_id": "1257",
        "explanation": "The senator announces a local firefighting grant and links it directly to his personal success in writing, passing, and funding the FIRE Act."
      },
      {
        "document_id": "2697",
        "explanation": "The senator claims credit for personally intervening to find an alternative federal funding source after the state's initial emergency request was denied."
      }
    ],
    "negative_clarification": "Do NOT classify a text as credit-claiming (0) if the senator is merely: 1) Introducing or co-sponsoring a bill or amendment that has not yet passed (which is policy advocacy). 2) Explaining their votes or expressing a general opinion on an issue (which is position-taking). 3) Conducting standard oversight, such as writing letters or questioning witnesses, without achieving a finalized outcome. 4) Issuing tributes, condolences, or scheduling announcements (which are forms of advertising or symbolic politics).",
    "negative_examples": [
      {
        "document_id": "57",
        "explanation": "The senator is introducing a new piece of legislation. Sponsoring or introducing bills that have not yet passed constitutes policy advocacy rather than credit-claiming."
      },
